From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Fri Dec 13 20:07:06 2013
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Fri, 13 Dec 2013 20:07:06 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Model comparison through hierarchical specification in Stan
Message-ID: <UBR1k0bTJmE.ybU_M-NnDTYJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <UBR1k0bTJmE.7jVncFPHX4oJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <UBR1k0bTJmE.7jVncFPHX4oJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/UBR1k0bTJmE/m/ybU_M-NnDTYJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

That's a large model to try to diagnose.

My suggestion would be to do a line-for-line translation
and work from there.  For instance, I don't see the equivalent
of this piece of JAGS code in your Stan model:

   for (j in 1:nCond){
       aBeta[j] <- mu[j]     * (kappa[j]*equals(mdlIdx,1)+kappa0*equals(mdlIdx,2))
       bBeta[j] <- (1-mu[j]) * (kappa[j]*equals(mdlIdx,1)+kappa0*equals(mdlIdx,2))
   }

Alternatively, try a much simpler model and build up from
there.

We didn't really build Stan with model comparison in mind.
There are a couple of issues:

1.  Stan's reported lp__ values are the log probability on the
unconstrained parameters, which is the model log probability
plus the log Jacobians of the transforms (described in a
later chapter in the Stan manual).  Your message reminded me we
want to break that down, so I created a feature request:

https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/issues/448

2.  Stan's reported lp__ values drop any additive constants
on the log scale (multiplicative constants on the linear scale)
that arise from sampling statements.  You can get the full
log probabilities at the cost of some efficiency by replacing:

    y ~ foo(theta);

with

   increment_log_prob(foo_log(y,theta));

The latter includes the normalizing constants.

- Bob

On 12/12/13, 12:21 PM, Nathan Eustis Rutenbeck wrote:
> Hi Stan users,
>
> Thanks in advance for your help to a relatively new student of Bayesian methods and the use of Stan, and apologies for
> the long message.
>
> I am a forest ecology PhD student currently taking a course on Bayesian methods at the University of Maine using John
> Kruschke's 2011 'Doing Bayesian Data Analysis' textbook. Though I like the book overall, one of the downsides (for me
> personally) has been that a lot of the programming tasks have already been accomplished for the student. Therefore for
> my own edification in both the theoretical and programming aspects of Bayesian analysis I am attempting to translate
> some of the JAGS models Kruschke uses into Stan (I am working with rstan and R2jags).
>
> So far this has been relatively straightforward with one exception. One approach that Kruschke emphasizes is model
> comparison through hierarchical specification with different parameters indexed according to model as a way to estimate
> the posterior probabilities of the two models and thereby compare them. A good example of this is his hierarchical
> binomial 'Filtration - Condensation' model. A slightly modified data description and JAGS model that Kruschke specifies
> are as follows.
>
> data <- list(z = nCorrOfSubj, # number of successes in Bernoulli trials
>               n = nTrlOfSubj, # number of trials
>               cond = CondOfSubj, # condition of subject
>               nSubj = length(CondOfSubj), # total number of subjects
>               nCond = length(unique(CondOfSubj))) # total number of conditions
>
> jm1 <- function(){
>    # Likelihood
>    for (i in 1:nSubj){
>      z[i] ~ dbin(theta[i], n[i])
>      theta[i] ~ dbeta(aBeta[cond[i]], bBeta[cond[i]])
>    }
>    # Prior
>    for (j in 1:nCond){
>        aBeta[j] <- mu[j]     * (kappa[j]*equals(mdlIdx,1)+kappa0*equals(mdlIdx,2))
>        bBeta[j] <- (1-mu[j]) * (kappa[j]*equals(mdlIdx,1)+kappa0*equals(mdlIdx,2))
>    }
>
>    # Hyperprior on mu and kappa:
>    kappa0 ~ dgamma( shk0[mdlIdx] , rak0[mdlIdx] )
>    for ( j in 1:nCond ) {
>      mu[j] ~ dbeta( aHyperbeta , bHyperbeta )
>      kappa[j] ~ dgamma( shk[j,mdlIdx] , rak[j,mdlIdx] )
>    }
>    # Constants for hyperprior:
>    aHyperbeta <- 1
>    bHyperbeta <- 1
>    # Actual priors:
>    shP <- 1.0 # shape for prior
>    raP <- 0.1 # rate for prior
>    # shape, rate kappa0[ model ]
>    shk0[2] <- shP
>    rak0[2] <- raP
>    # shape kappa[ condition , model ]
>    shk[1,1] <- shP
>    shk[2,1] <- shP
>    shk[3,1] <- shP
>    shk[4,1] <- shP
>    # rate kappa[ condition , model ]
>    rak[1,1] <- raP
>    rak[2,1] <- raP
>    rak[3,1] <- raP
>    rak[4,1] <- raP
>    # Pseudo priors:
>    # shape, rate kappa0[ model ]
>    shk0[1] <- 54.0
>    rak0[1] <- 4.35
>    # shape kappa[ condition , model ]
>    shk[1,2] <- 11.8
>    shk[2,2] <- 11.9
>    shk[3,2] <- 13.6
>    shk[4,2] <- 12.6
>    # rate kappa[ condition , model ]
>    rak[1,2] <- 1.34
>    rak[2,2] <- 1.11
