>  Writing code to write my data into the R-dump format was pretty straight-forward and didn't require me to know anything about R.    Writing out my data in JSON, however, would be significantly more difficult and require me to learn about the intricacies of a moderately convoluted data format (at least relative to R-dump).

I think most people would argue the exact other way around. JSON is a very clean, super simple, widely adopted data format that can easily be generated and parsed in almost every language, without any manual string manipulation. Both Python and R have packages to convert R objects to JSON, so if you're already familiar with R, it would be one line of code.

Have a look at the (relatively new) R package jsonlite, which comes with a vignette that describes a precise mapping between json data and R objects: http://cran.rstudio.com/web/packages/jsonlite/vignettes/json-mapping.pdf

> An old-fashioned comma (or tab) delimited file with variable names in the top row (data.csv) may have disadvantages in this setting that I don't grasp,

It's limited to structures in a tabular format. Not all data fits in tables.

