I agree.  Sending in data (and, for that matter, getting out inferences) as a list of objects seems like the best way to go.  One of the frustrations of Excel, State, etc., is that they can lead to people trying to shoehorn data into a rectangular format.  For Stan, we're passing N, X, y, etc etc, and we're getting inference for scalars, vectors, arrays, etc.  In R, it works fine because we either pass a list of data or a vector of names of data objects.  As an R user, I'm not so concerned about what format Stan uses internally, all that matters to me is that I can pass R objects to it.  If those objects need to be translated to Jason or whatever, that's fine.
A

