You could always write it as a log-linear model instead of
a linear model --- just exp() transform the linear
predictor.

Your code:

   for (i in 1:trialNumTotal)
     thisSigma[i] <- personSigma[personNum[i]] + conditionContrast[i] * personSigmaEffect[personNum[i]];

   y ~ normal(0, thisSigma);

would change on the second line to this:

   y ~ normal(0, exp(thisSigma));

You don't need a Jacobian adjustment for parameter transforms on the
right-hand side of sampling statements.

You can then get rid of all the constraints on the slope
and intercept in the regression for thisSigma.

Alternatively, you could tranform the predictor conditionContrast[i]
if you really want personSigma and personSigmaEffect to be positive.

- Bob

On 12/24/13, 9:40 PM, Mike Lawrence wrote:
> Hi folks,
>
> I'm having trouble figuring out the best way to code models where there is a hierarchical structure on parameters that
> need to be constrained. The simplest example I could come up with is here:
> https://gist.github.com/mike-lawrence/8119696
>
> In that model, data are generated from a gaussian with mean of zero and standard deviation ("sigma") that is influenced
> by two variables, "participant" and "condition". There are 100 levels of participant and 2 levels of condition. Sigma
> values vary across participants according to a gaussian, and the effect of condition on sigma values also varies across
> participants according to a gaussian.
>
> Ideally the model should be set up so that it only explores areas of the parameter space that yield positive values for
> sigma, but despite my efforts thus far, I can't seem to figure out how to achieve this constraint. The result is
